{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And it was on the next day.</b> Rashi explains: on the next day after Yom HaKippurim, the next day after the day of his descent from the mountain. And therefore it can only be the next day after Yom HaKippurim, for before the giving of the Torah it is impossible to say ״והודעתי״ etc., and from when the Torah was given until Yom HaKippurim he did not sit to judge the people, for on the seventeenth of Tammuz he broke the tablets, and the next morning he went up early and stayed there forty days and descended on Yom HaKippurim. And this passage is not written in order, for it was not said until the second year, since it says here ״וישלח משה את חותנו״, and we find in the journey of the standards that Moses said to him ״נוסעים אנחנו, אל נא תעזוב אותנו״ — and if this was before the giving of the Torah, once he sent him off and he went, where do we find that he returned? And if you ask: there it does not say יתרו but חובב, and it was Jethro's son — חובב is Jethro, as it is written ״מבני חובב חותן משה״. Thus far Rashi. And there is a difficulty: if so, it implies that at the time Moses descended from the mountain — that is, on Yom HaKippurim — he went out to meet his father-in-law, and at that same time ״ויקח יתרו עולה וזבחים״ and they ate on that very day, Yom HaKippurim — and how could they eat, when the Torah had already been given? And furthermore, it is difficult according to the one who says Jethro came before the giving of the Torah. And one may answer that this act of offering sacrifices perhaps did not take place on the day of Moses' descent from the mountain, but rather on the second day or the third day. And even though the verse ״ויבא משה״ stands before this verse ״ויהי ממחרת,״ it refers only to the next day after Yom HaKippurim, and the act of eating was on that same day as well; while his going out to meet him was on day one, and what they ate with him was on another day. And all this can be said according to Rashi's position, who explains that Jethro came after the giving of the Torah. But according to the one who says Jethro came before the giving of the Torah, one may answer that it is possible that this act — that Moses sat to judge the people — was before the giving of the Torah, for Rashi himself, of blessed memory, explained in the portion of בשלח that at Marah they were commanded regarding civil laws. And if so, one may say that they ate with him on the day of his arrival. And in the Midrash Tanchuma, it interprets that Jethro came after the giving of the Torah, and expounds upon it this verse: ״לב יודע מרת נפשו״ — and in his joy no stranger may mingle. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: My children were in the bondage of Egypt, and Jethro was sitting tranquil and secure; it is only fitting that he rejoice in the joy of the Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:31.108308490Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}